<description>
Fill zero holes by reconstructing the smallest repeating color table visible in the grid.

Input structure
The grid is a rectangular periodic color table with black 0 cells as missing entries; each position in the true repeating tile is visible at least once.

Rule
Infer the smallest period p where all nonzero cells agree by (row mod p, column mod p), build that p x p tile, and replace every cell by the corresponding tile color.

Key insight
The nonzero pixels are samples of one complete repeating tile, so the repair follows from periodicity rather than from numeric color ordering.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "nonzero pixels of a grid with black 0 holes, sampled from one complete smallest repeating color table",
  "transform": "infer the smallest period whose row/column residues agree for all visible pixels, reconstruct that tile, and fill every 0 cell from the periodic repetition"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: always 18x18, input == output size
  tile:
    period_p: 5-9, one value per puzzle
    formula: |
      tile[r][c] = 1 if r==0 or c==0, else (r*c mod p) + 1
      Colors used: 1..p. First row/col all 1s.
      This is a shifted multiplication table mod p.
    tile_is_deterministic: fully determined by p alone
  input:
    structure: output (the full periodic tiling) with 5 rectangular holes zeroed out
    holes: exactly 5 axis-aligned rectangles, each dimension 1-6, area 4-16
    zero_fraction: 8-16% of grid cells
    coverage: every tile position (r%p, c%p) must remain visible (non-zero) at least once
    rectangles_may_touch: adjacent rects can share edges, forming non-rectangular connected components
  output: full periodic tiling of the p x p tile across the 18x18 grid, no zeros

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick p uniformly from 5-9
    2. Build p x p tile via formula tile[r][c] = 1 if r==0 or c==0 else (r*c)%p + 1
    3. Tile it across 18x18 to get the output grid
    4. Place 5 non-overlapping rectangles as holes (dims 1-6 each side, area 4-16)
    5. Zero out hole cells to get the input grid
    6. Verify every tile position (r%p, c%p) has >=1 non-zero witness; reject and retry step 4 if not
  notes: |
    Coverage check in step 6 is cheap. For small p (5-6) the grid has many
    tile repetitions so coverage almost always holds. For p=9 (4 reps per
    tile position), place holes more carefully or retry.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

